{
  "summary": "This computer looks in good shape: all six findings are low severity and most look like normal setups for a home PC. The one thing worth a moment of your time is confirming you recognize the second administrator account, <computer>\\<account-2>. After that, glance over the startup apps (Discord, Spotify, Notion) and the HP dock updater to make sure they're things you actually use.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Administrator accounts on this computer",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291",
        "ACCT-DAILY-ADMIN:44c65344"
      ],
      "explanation": "Two accounts can change anything on this computer: your everyday account (<computer>\\<user>) and a second one, <computer>\\<account-2>. Having a separate admin account is actually good practice, so if you created <computer>\\<account-2> yourself or know who did, that's fine. An admin account you don't recognize, though, is a common sign someone else got in, so please confirm you know it. Separately, your everyday account is itself an administrator. That's very common on home PCs, but it means anything you run, including something harmful opened by mistake, can get full control after you click through a UAC prompt. The report shows the suggested fix for each if you want to tighten this up."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "Apps that start automatically when you sign in",
      "finding_ids": [
        "PERSIST-REVIEW:46fde10b",
        "PERSIST-REVIEW:a6556492",
        "PERSIST-REVIEW:422bd707"
      ],
      "explanation": "Discord, Spotify and Notion are set to start when you log in. They run from your personal AppData folder, which programs can write to without admin rights. That's why the scan flags them, but it's exactly how these apps normally install themselves. If you use these three apps, there's nothing to worry about. If you don't recognize one, check the report's suggested fix for that entry."
    },
    {
      "priority": 3,
      "title": "HP dock firmware updater set to run on a system event",
      "finding_ids": [
        "WMI-CONSUMER-REVIEW:7d52aadf"
      ],
      "explanation": "A background Windows trigger (a WMI event subscription) runs HP's firmware installer for the HP USB-C Dock G5. The program lives in Program Files, where only admins can add files, and the scan found nothing suspicious about it. This is how some dock firmware updaters work. If you use an HP USB-C dock, it's expected. If you don't, see the report's suggested fix."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": []
}
